@meta
id: 10-02
title: Where the space goes
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-word, inner-vs-around
par: 24
minutes: 5
vscode: select the word plus its trailing space by hand, delete
open: order.resolver.ts

@brief
Every text object comes in two flavours. `i` is inner: the thing itself,
nothing else. `a` is around: the thing plus what holds it apart from its
neighbours. For a word, "what holds it apart" means one space.

That sounds like a footnote until you delete something. Take a word out
of the middle of a line with the inner object and you are left with two
spaces where there was one. Nobody wants that, and nobody notices it
until the diff.

The lint rule `@typescript-eslint/explicit-member-accessibility` has been
turned off and `public` is now redundant noise on every method in the
codebase. Take it off all three methods in this resolver. Indentation
stays exactly as it is, and `async` stays.

If your result has a double space in it, you picked the wrong flavour.

@keys
| `diw` | delete the word, leave the whitespace alone |
| `daw` | delete the word and one space with it |
| `ciw` `caw` | the same distinction for change |

@hints
There are two word objects, not one. Only one of them leaves the line
looking like a human wrote it.
--
`aw` is "a word": the word plus the space that follows it. `daw` is what
you want here.
--
Get onto the word first — `daw` from the indent in front of it deletes
the indent instead. `w` from the start of the line lands on `public`.

@solution
    7j w daw
    4j daw
    4j daw
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty keystrokes for three edits, and notice what is not in there: no
counting of characters, no `$`, no visual selection.

The rule for `aw` is worth memorising because it is not symmetrical. It
takes the whitespace *after* the word; only if there is none does it fall
back to the whitespace before. So `daw` on the last word of a line eats
the space in front of it, which is right, and `daw` in the middle of a
line eats the space behind it, which is also right. Vim is doing the
thing you would have done by hand.

`dw` would have worked here too, but only because you were standing on
the `p`. Land one letter late and `dw` deletes `ublic ` and you have to
undo. `daw` does not care. Every keystroke you do not have to aim is a
keystroke that survives being tired.

Counts work as well: `d3aw` takes three words and their spaces, which is
the fastest way to shorten a sentence.

@start order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }

  public async refundOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.refund(args.id);
  }
}

@expected order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }

  async refundOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.refund(args.id);
  }
}
